\details{
For a sample \eqn{x} (with the pseudocount added) and part weights
\eqn{p}, write \eqn{y_j = x_j / p_j}. At an internal node with clades
\eqn{A} (its first child, the numerator) and \eqn{B} (its second), let
\eqn{n_A = \sum_{j \in A} p_j} and \eqn{\log g_A = \sum_{j \in A} p_j
\log y_j / n_A} (and likewise for \eqn{B}). The node's coordinate is
\deqn{f \sqrt{\frac{n_A n_B}{n_A + n_B}}\,\left(\log g_A - \log g_B\right),}
with \eqn{f} its ILR weight (Silverman et al. 2017, Methods). It is
computed in \eqn{O(D)} per sample, by summing over each clade from the
tips up, without forming the \eqn{D \times (D - 1)} basis.

With uniform weights the coordinates are an ordinary ilr: they equal
\code{ilr(counts, basis = sbp)} for the tree's sequential binary partition, and
\code{euclidean(philr(x, tree))} is \code{aitchison(x)} (with the same
pseudocount), as for any orthonormal basis. It is the weights that make
distances phylogenetic: \code{\link[=philr_distance]{philr_distance()}} uses the paper's.

The coordinates feed straight into \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}, \code{\link[=rda]{rda()}}, \code{\link[=lda]{lda()}}, \code{\link[=envfit]{envfit()}}
and \code{\link[=euclidean]{euclidean()}}, which take negative values with \code{norm = 'none'}.

\code{philr(x, tree, pseudocount = c, part_weights = pw, ilr_weights = iw)}
equals philr 1.28.0's \code{philr(x, tree, part.weights = pw, ilr.weights = iw, pseudocount = c)} to rounding, for every weighting and for a binary tree
whose tips are the features of \code{x}.
}
\section{The tree}{

PhILR needs a rooted binary tree whose tips are the features. \code{tree} is
made one in three steps (the result is the \code{tree} attribute):
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Tips that aren't features of \code{counts} are dropped}, and so are the
internal nodes left without any. Every feature must be a tip.
\item \strong{Nodes left with one child are removed}, their branch length added
to their child's (a root with one child is dropped), as
\code{ape::drop.tip()} does.
\item \strong{Polytomies are resolved, with a warning:} a node with children
\eqn{c_1, \dots, c_k}, \eqn{k > 2}, keeps \eqn{c_1} and gets a new
child node, joined by a zero-length branch, that holds \eqn{c_2, \dots,
   c_k} (and so on), as \code{ape::multi2di(random = FALSE)} does. An unrooted
tree (three children at the root) is resolved the same way. Results
then depend on the order of the children: to choose the resolution,
resolve the tree first.
}

Coordinates are in the order of the internal nodes' numbers in \code{tree}
(the root first), or, when a step changed the tree, in preorder of the
new tree (which is also the order of \code{ape::multi2di()}'s and
\code{ape::drop.tip()}'s numbering). Branch lengths are needed only for the
branch-length ILR weights; they must be non-negative. A root edge is
ignored.

\strong{Same names in other packages}: \code{philr::philr()} computes the same
transform, with the same orientation and order. Whichever package was
attached last masks the other's \code{philr()}; write \code{ecodive::philr()} to be
sure.
}

\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\section{Pseudocount}{

Logs of zero are undefined, so zeros need replacing first. The
pseudocount is added to every value (not only to zeros), as in
\code{normalize(norm = 'clr')}. With \code{pseudocount = NULL} (the default), no
pseudocount is used when \code{counts} has no zeros; otherwise half the
smallest non-zero value is, with a warning. To avoid a pseudocount,
replace the zeros first with \code{\link[=replace_zeros]{replace_zeros()}}: its result has none.

The choice of pseudocount is not neutral: it can distort the results,
especially for sparse tables. See \code{\link[=aitchison]{aitchison()}} for references.
}

\section{Machine learning}{

Log-ratio transforms (clr, rclr, alr, ilr, PhILR) serve interpretation
and compositional geometry; don't expect them to improve prediction.
With random forests predicting 65 metadata variables in four datasets,
Yerke et al. (2024) found that alr, clr and ilr (PhILR) "performed
generally slightly worse or only as well as compositionally naïve
transformations", and relative abundances did best, by a small margin.
Compare with \code{norm = 'percent'} (see \code{\link[=normalize]{normalize()}}) before choosing a
log-ratio predictor table.
}

\examples{
    x <- philr(ex_counts, tree = ex_tree, pseudocount = 1)
    dim(x)
    x[1:3, 1:4]

    # With uniform weights, distances are Aitchison distances
    all.equal(
      as.vector(euclidean(x)),
      as.vector(aitchison(ex_counts, pseudocount = 1)) )

    # The paper's weights
    x <- philr(
      ex_counts, tree = ex_tree, pseudocount = 1,
      part_weights = 'enorm.x.gm.counts', ilr_weights = 'blw.sqrt' )
    head(attr(x, 'ilr_weights'))

}
